\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Two quaternionic links remove the rational kernel}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Use the one-vertex quaternionic connection of \ref{fdip-00SN}
with the invariant norm pairing
#{b(u,v)=\operatorname{Re}(\bar u v)}. Set #{a=-\qi-1} and
#{b_0=-\qj-1}; a quaternion in the following matrices acts by
left multiplication. The even-to-odd block is
##{B=\begin{pmatrix}a&-\bar b_0\\b_0&\bar a\end{pmatrix}.}
Write #{c=ab_0-b_0a=2\qk}. Quaternionic conjugate transpose gives
##{B^\dagger B=4\bbI_2+C,\qquad
C=\begin{pmatrix}0&\bar c\\c&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad C^2=4\bbI_2.}
Indeed the diagonal entries are
#{\bar a a+\bar b_0 b_0=4}, the lower off-diagonal entry is
#{-b_0a+ab_0=c}, and the upper is its conjugate.}

\p{Consequently
##{(4\bbI_2+C)^{-1}=\frac{4\bbI_2-C}{12}.}
If #{Bv=0}, then #{B^\dagger Bv=0}, so #{v=0}. Over
#{\mathbb Q}, equal finite dimensions now make #{B} bijective;
its adjoint is bijective as well. The parity block form
#{\extD_U=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&B^\dagger\\B&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)}
is therefore invertible. At trivial links, #{a=b_0=0}, so
#{\extD_U=0} on the same #{16}-dimensional rational space.}

\p{This uses the rational norm pairing. Stability of the
Hurwitz lattice under the links does not make that norm pairing
integrally unimodular, and the division by #{12} is not
available modulo #{2} or #{3}.}
